WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.417
Performance and security are connected in data-heavy applications.

2
00:00:04.417 --> 00:00:16.863
A query that reads too broadly, loads too many related entities, exports too much data, or runs without limits can create availability and privacy problems even when it is parameterized.

3
00:00:16.863 --> 00:00:19.874
Bounded data access is part of secure design.

4
00:00:19.874 --> 00:00:25.361
Use pagination, filtered queries, reasonable date ranges, and bounded result sets.

5
00:00:25.361 --> 00:00:32.321
These controls reduce accidental full-table reads, limit exposure, and protect shared database capacity.

6
00:00:32.321 --> 00:00:38.879
They also make application behavior more predictable for users, operators, and downstream systems.

7
00:00:38.879 --> 00:00:43.362
Be careful with includes, lazy loading, and relationship traversal.

8
00:00:43.362 --> 00:00:51.994
Loading related data can be necessary, but it can also create unexpected data access, performance spikes, or accidental exposure.

9
00:00:51.994 --> 00:00:59.288
Developers should understand what each query loads and whether the caller is authorized to receive that data.

10
00:00:59.288 --> 00:01:03.839
Unbounded exports, reports, and administrative views deserve review.

11
00:01:03.839 --> 00:01:10.597
These workflows may be legitimate, but they often touch broad data and run under privileged contexts.

12
00:01:10.597 --> 00:01:18.293
They should have clear filters, resource limits, authorization checks, audit records, and operational expectations.

13
00:01:18.293 --> 00:01:25.118
Timeouts, cancellation tokens, retries, and circuit-breaking behavior should be designed deliberately.

14
00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:30.605
Retrying database operations without understanding failure mode can increase load.

15
00:01:30.605 --> 00:01:35.222
Ignoring cancellation can waste resources after a caller disconnects.

16
00:01:35.222 --> 00:01:42.516
Timeouts and cancellation help the application fail predictably rather than slowly consuming shared capacity.

17
00:01:42.516 --> 00:01:48.873
Background jobs and batch operations should follow the same data access rules as request paths.

18
00:01:48.873 --> 00:01:55.900
They need tenant context, authorization assumptions, resource limits, logging, and safe failure handling.

19
00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:03.394
Operational resilience means failures are observable, recoverable, and safe for data integrity and availability.
